# `M157f` / `C48` — `teardown`, the config key that decides when a workload's `after` hooks run.
#
# **This file is meant to produce two red tests**, and both reds are its known answer rather than a
# defect. `verify-construct-acceptance.mjs` reads `report/results.json`, not the exit status.
#
# ## What this replaces, and why the plant changed shape
#
# `C48` graded `cleanup` until `M157`: a bare per-test line that opted a workload back into running
# the file's `after` hooks, off by default. `M154e-01` was filed against it because `tflw spec --json`
# described that construct wrongly in three separate ways — and the row was deliberately *not* fixed
# in place, on the grounds that the repair was a choice between two readings of what `cleanup` was
# for and `D26` argued for the implemented one.
#
# `M157` settled it by measuring `D26` rather than by picking a reading. `D26` justified the gate as
# protecting a run's latency numbers from teardown's request volume; three 20-iteration arms on the
# box put an **ungated `before` hook** at the same 96 ms p95 as the gated `after` hook, against a
# 37 ms control. The gate protected the rare side and left the common one open. So the keyword is
# gone (`D781`), hook time left the reported duration entirely (`D782`), and the behaviour the gate
# provided moved to a three-valued config key (`D783`).
#
# ## The plant: one file, three runs, three different answers
#
# The contrast IS the plant, and it is now three-way rather than two-way. `/after-each-marker` is
# hit once per iteration whose teardown runs, so the marker count answers the whole question:
#
#   - **default** (`teardown always`) -> **8**. Every iteration of both tests, including the four
#     that failed. That last part is the half `M154e-01` did not know about: until `M157b` the
#     interpreter threw on a failing body *above* the hook loop, so a run at a 5% error rate cleaned
#     up after 95% of its iterations and leaked on precisely the 5% worth investigating.
#   - **`--teardown never`** -> **0**, with `ℹ teardown: disabled` on the summary.
#   - **`--teardown on success`** -> **4**. Only the first test's iterations, because only they
#     passed.
#
# A build that ignored the key answers 8 three times; one that reinstated the old gate answers 0
# three times, since no file can opt in any more.
#
# ## The hook is deliberately expensive (`M157g`)
#
# `/after-each-marker` sleeps 50 ms, matching `/slow`, and that is load-bearing rather than
# incidental. The fourth clause the grader reads is a **null result** — `D782` says hook time leaves
# the reported iteration duration, so the p95 must not move as the hook count goes 8 -> 0 -> 4 — and
# a null result only means something if the movement it denies would have been visible. Against a
# zero-latency marker the denied effect was one local request, smaller than the jitter it was being
# compared under. Now a build that put hook time back into the duration moves the p95 by ~50 ms per
# iteration and the clause can fail for its own reason.
#
# ## The sharp clause: `on success` reads the ITERATION, not the run
#
# The first test below is **red**, and every one of its four iterations **passes**. Its threshold is
# `error rate is less than 0%`, which nothing can satisfy — so the test's verdict is red while its
# iterations are green. Under `--teardown on success` all four still tear down, because a breached
# `threshold` is a *run*-level judgement decided after every iteration has already finished, and
# therefore after teardown has already run. A build that read the test's verdict instead of the
# iteration's would answer 0 here and look plausible doing it.

after
  api GET /after-each-marker
  expect status equals 200

test "an iteration that passed tears down, even though the test is red by threshold"
  run 4 iterations across 1 users
  api GET /teardown-pass
  expect status equals 200
  threshold error rate is less than 0%

test "an iteration that failed tears down too — the leak M154e-01 did not know it had"
  run 4 iterations across 1 users
  api GET /teardown-fail
  expect status equals 500
  threshold error rate is less than 1%
